SUMMARY:From Ancient Americas to Ancestral Abiayala
DESCRIPTION:From Ancient Americas to Ancestral Abiayala            \n                                    \n                                \n                        \n                                                \n                                                        Get Tickets                        \n                                            \n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n	\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n	\n\n\n			\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Thursday October 22\, 2026\n6:00 pm – 8:00 pm\n \nThe Diane Wolfe Lecture \nSpeaker: Juliana Ramírez Herrera\, Ph.D.\, Assistant Professor of Indigenous Art and Archaeology of the Ancestral Americas and of Colonial Latin American Art\, University of California\, Berkeley \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n             \n                \n                    Join Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera\, curator of the Gardiner Museum’s Ancestral Abiayala Gallery—formerly Ancient Americas—for a conversation on repatriation\, broadly understood\, and how it shaped her vision for the renovated space. \nDrawing on other threads of her research\, Dr. Ramírez Herrera will situate Ancestral Abiayala within larger conversations about the mobility of belongings\, violent and otherwise\, and what it might mean to care for them responsibly in museum practice and scholarly writing. \nThe talk offers a behind-the-scenes look at what went into developing the Ancestral Abiayala Gallery\, connecting its questions to broader intellectual debates in the field. \n\n                \n                    \n    \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n			\n\n        \n          \n          \n                        \n               \n                General : $25\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Gardiner Friends : $21\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Patron Members : Free\n                                        \n                        \n               \n                Students : Free\n                                        \n                      \n\n           \n            \n              Gardiner Friends\, log in to access your discount.* \nNot a Gardiner Friend yet? Join now at the Friend level or above to save . \n*Discounts on programs and classes do not apply to ‘Annual Pass’ level. \n            \n                    \n          \n            \n  \n            \n                                Get Tickets\n              \n                        \n           \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n          \n          \n        \n\n	\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n    \n         \n    \n\n\n\n\n    \n        \n                            \n                    \n                        \n                    \n                \n                       \n            \n                \n                    About the Speaker\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera\n                    \n                \n                \n                    Dr. Juliana Ramírez Herrera is Assistant Professor of Indigenous Art and Archaeology of the Ancestral Americas and of Colonial Latin American Art at the University of California\, Berkeley. She specializes in the so-called small-scale societies of pre-Conquest and early colonial Panama\, Colombia\, the Caribbean\, and Amazonia. Before joining UC Berkeley\, she was a SSHRC Postdoctoral Fellow at the University of Toronto and the Gardiner Museum of Ceramic Art\, where she collaborated with curators Dr. Sequoia Miller\, Dr. Karine Tsoumis\, and Franchesca Herbert-Spence on the renovation of the Museum’s ground floor. She earned her PhD from Harvard University and her MA from the University of Toronto. \nPhoto: Julie Wolf \n                            \n            \n        \n    \n\n\n\n    \n         
URL:https://www.gardinermuseum.on.ca/event/from-ancient-americas-to-ancestral-abiayala/
